BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A dye house''s technical team decides to switch a specific reactive dyeing recipe from a HIGH-reactivity dye (fast fixation, but more prone to hydrolysis/wasted dye if timing isn''t precise) to a LOWER-reactivity dye (slower fixation, more forgiving timing, but requiring higher fixation temperature/longer time to achieve equivalent fixation%). For a mill with INCONSISTENT process timing control (older machines, variable operator practices), which dye reactivity category is the SAFER choice, and why?', '["High-reactivity dye is always the safer choice for any mill regardless of process timing consistency, since faster fixation is unconditionally better in every situation","Lower-reactivity dye is generally the safer choice for a mill with inconsistent timing control, since its more forgiving fixation kinetics (slower, less sensitive to precise timing) make it less vulnerable to the yield/shade-consistency problems that timing variability would cause with a fast, timing-sensitive high-reactivity dye — this directly connects to the earlier reactive-dye-sequencing discussion, where premature/imprecisely-timed alkali addition caused yield loss specifically WORSENED by a fast-reacting dye''s greater sensitivity to that exact timing","Dye reactivity category has no relationship to a mill''s process timing consistency requirements, making this entire selection consideration technically irrelevant","Both dye reactivity categories are equally forgiving of timing inconsistency, making reactivity level an irrelevant selection factor for any mill regardless of its process control capability"]'::jsonb, 'Lower-reactivity dye is generally the safer choice for a mill with inconsistent timing control, since its more forgiving fixation kinetics (slower, less sensitive to precise timing) make it less vulnerable to the yield/shade-consistency problems that timing variability would cause with a fast, timing-sensitive high-reactivity dye — this directly connects to the earlier reactive-dye-sequencing discussion, where premature/imprecisely-timed alkali addition caused yield loss specifically WORSENED by a fast-reacting dye''s greater sensitivity to that exact timing', 'This directly extends the earlier reactive-dye-sequencing/timing-sensitivity discussion into a practical dye-selection decision — a HIGH-reactivity dye''s fast fixation is attractive for PRODUCTIVITY (shorter process time) but makes shade/yield outcomes MORE sensitive to precise process timing (exactly the sensitivity the earlier premature-alkali-addition scenario demonstrated); for a mill genuinely unable to guarantee consistent, precise timing control, choosing a LOWER-reactivity dye trades away some productivity for genuinely more FORGIVING, robust process behavior, reducing the risk of yield loss/shade inconsistency from real-world timing variability — matching dye chemistry selection to the mill''s actual process control capability, not just choosing based on theoretical productivity alone.', 3, '{"dye-reactivity-process-robustness"}'::text[]),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A finishing plant''s technical team notices that a specific softener formulation gives excellent initial hand-feel but the softness effect largely DISAPPEARS after just 2-3 wash cycles, while a different (more expensive) softener maintains its effect through 15+ wash cycles. What does this durability DIFFERENCE most likely indicate about the two softeners'' mechanism of attachment to the fiber?', '["Softener durability has no relationship to its mechanism of attachment to fiber, making this observed durability difference purely coincidental with no underlying chemical explanation","The less-durable softener likely relies on relatively weak physical deposition/surface coating (easily washed/rinsed away with repeated laundering, similar in principle to how loosely-bound, unfixed dye washes out as discussed in the earlier reactive-dye-fixation context), while the more durable, wash-resistant softener likely achieves a stronger chemical/molecular attachment to the fiber surface (e.g., through reactive bonding or strong ionic/hydrophobic interaction) that resists being washed away as easily","Both softeners must use mathematically identical attachment mechanisms, making any difference in wash durability technically impossible to explain through mechanism differences","Softener durability is determined entirely by the softener''s COLOR, with zero relationship to its actual chemical attachment mechanism to the fiber"]'::jsonb, 'The less-durable softener likely relies on relatively weak physical deposition/surface coating (easily washed/rinsed away with repeated laundering, similar in principle to how loosely-bound, unfixed dye washes out as discussed in the earlier reactive-dye-fixation context), while the more durable, wash-resistant softener likely achieves a stronger chemical/molecular attachment to the fiber surface (e.g., through reactive bonding or strong ionic/hydrophobic interaction) that resists being washed away as easily', 'This applies the general ''attachment mechanism strength determines wash durability'' principle (already established for dye fixation — weak physical/hydrogen-bond attachment washes out more easily than strong covalent bonding) to a finishing-chemical context — a softener that loses its effect quickly likely relies on weaker physical surface deposition (easily rinsed/washed away, similar to unfixed/hydrolyzed dye), while a wash-durable softener likely achieves genuinely stronger fiber attachment (through more reactive or strongly-interacting chemistry) that resists laundering removal — this same underlying principle (attachment mechanism strength predicts wash durability) applies broadly across MANY different textile chemical treatments, not just dye.', 3, '{"softener-durability-mechanism"}'::text[]),
('33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A wet processing plant compares ''enzyme washing'' (using cellulase enzymes for denim fading/softening, as discussed earlier) against traditional ''stone washing'' (using pumice stones for the same fading effect) on fabric TENSILE STRENGTH impact. Both achieve visually similar fading, but enzyme washing generally causes LESS tensile strength LOSS than stone washing for an equivalent visual fading effect. Why?', '["Enzyme washing and stone washing cause mathematically identical strength loss for any equivalent fading level, making this described difference technically incorrect","Stone washing achieves fading through PURELY MECHANICAL abrasion (physical stone-on-fabric impact/friction, which indiscriminately damages fiber structure throughout the abraded area, including undamaged, still-useful fiber), while enzyme washing achieves fading through a more SELECTIVE chemical/biological mechanism (cellulase enzymes preferentially attacking loose, protruding surface fibrils rich in surface indigo, as established earlier) — this selectivity means enzyme washing removes primarily the SPECIFIC surface material responsible for visual color while causing proportionally less indiscriminate damage to the fabric''s core structural fiber, achieving comparable fading with less overall strength loss","Stone washing always causes LESS strength loss than enzyme washing for any equivalent fading level, the opposite of the actual, well-documented relationship between these two fading methods","Tensile strength has no relationship to either fading method, making any comparison between their strength-loss impact technically meaningless"]'::jsonb, 'Stone washing achieves fading through PURELY MECHANICAL abrasion (physical stone-on-fabric impact/friction, which indiscriminately damages fiber structure throughout the abraded area, including undamaged, still-useful fiber), while enzyme washing achieves fading through a more SELECTIVE chemical/biological mechanism (cellulase enzymes preferentially attacking loose, protruding surface fibrils rich in surface indigo, as established earlier) — this selectivity means enzyme washing removes primarily the SPECIFIC surface material responsible for visual color while causing proportionally less indiscriminate damage to the fabric''s core structural fiber, achieving comparable fading with less overall strength loss', 'This extends the earlier enzyme-washing/biopolishing mechanism discussion with a direct mechanistic comparison to stone washing — stone washing''s PURELY MECHANICAL abrasion mechanism is inherently non-selective (physical impact damages whatever fiber structure it contacts, regardless of whether that fiber is ''excess'' surface fibril or core structural fiber), while enzyme washing''s biochemical mechanism SELECTIVELY targets the specific loose, protruding surface fibrils that carry most of the surface indigo color (as established earlier) — this selectivity is precisely why enzyme washing can achieve comparable visual fading with measurably LESS overall structural/tensile strength damage than the indiscriminate mechanical abrasion of stone washing, a genuine, well-documented industry advantage that has driven enzyme washing''s increasing adoption over pure stone washing.', 3, '{"enzyme-vs-stone-washing-strength"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
